# Client-Side API Caching Strategy

Avoid redundant API calls when navigating between pages by caching responses in memory (React state/context) and syncing to `localStorage` with a TTL. Data is only re-fetched from the API when the cache is stale or the page is hard-refreshed.

---

## How It Works

```
First visit to page  →  cache miss  →  fetch API  →  store in memory + localStorage (with timestamp)
Navigate away        →  component unmounts, memory cache lives in context
Return to page       →  cache hit (memory)  →  render instantly, NO API call
Hard refresh         →  memory wiped  →  check localStorage TTL
  └─ TTL valid       →  render from localStorage instantly, validate in background
  └─ TTL expired     →  fetch API, update cache
Mutation (add/edit/delete) → invalidate relevant cache key → next visit re-fetches
```

---

## Proposed Changes

### 1 — Core Cache Utility

#### [NEW] `frontend/src/utils/cache.js`

A lightweight utility for reading/writing TTL-stamped cache entries to `localStorage`.

```js
const CACHE_PREFIX = "urbanstay_cache_";
const DEFAULT_TTL_MS = 5 * 60 * 1000; // 5 minutes

export const cache = {
  set(key, data, ttlMs = DEFAULT_TTL_MS) { ... },
  get(key) { ... /* returns null if expired */ },
  invalidate(key) { ... },
  invalidateAll() { ... },
};
```

**TTL values by data type:**
| Data | Suggested TTL | Reasoning |
|---|---|---|
| Home property listing | 5 min | Changes infrequently |
| Host's own properties | 2 min | User may edit often |
| Cities list | 30 min | Very static |
| User profile | Permanent (session) | Already in AuthContext |

